-- title:
--   Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation of the coarse fake elliptic curve tower
-- statement:
--   Arithmetic data. Two distinct primes $r$ and $\bar r$ are fixed, together with a nonzero natural number $N$ that is squarefree and divisible by neither $r$ nor $\bar r$.
--
--   The base ring. $\mathcal O$ is a characteristic-zero domain that is a discrete valuation ring, in which $2$ and $3$ are units; $\pi \in \mathcal O$ is irreducible, $\mathcal O$ is complete for the $\pi$-adic topology (in the sense of `IsAdicComplete` for the ideal $(\pi)$), the residue ring $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ has exactly $r$ elements, and $(r) = (\pi)$, so that $\mathcal O$ is absolutely unramified with residue field $\mathbb F_r$; $K_0$ is a characteristic-zero field realised as a fraction field of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   The unramified covering data. $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ is a characteristic-zero domain which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra, $\mathrm{Fr}$ is an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism of it, and the following are assumed: $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ is complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`), that ideal is maximal (`hOnr_max`), every element of $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ satisfies some monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo that ideal (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ of positive degree has a root modulo that ideal (`hOnr_closed`, so the residue field is algebraically closed), and $\mathrm{Fr}(x) \equiv x^{r}$ modulo that ideal for all $x$ (`hFr`, so $\mathrm{Fr}$ lifts the Frobenius). Further, $v\!\det : \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0) \to \mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) is a monoid homomorphism characterised by `hvdet`: $v\!\det(g) = n$ if and only if $\det g = u\,\pi^{n}$ in $K_0$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   The indefinite quaternion algebra and the moduli scheme. For rationals $a, b$, the hypothesis `hB` states that $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q, a, b]$ satisfies $0 < a$ or $0 < b$ and that, for a finite place $v$ of $\mathbb Q$, the completion $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]\otimes_{\mathbb Q} \mathbb Q_v$ is a division algebra exactly when $v$ lies over $r$ or over $\bar r$. $\Lambda$ is an order in $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ maximal among orders. $f : \mathcal X \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ is a scheme over $\mathcal O$ and $\mathrm{pt}$ assigns, to every commutative ring $S$, every morphism $s : \operatorname{Spec} S \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and every fake elliptic curve of level $N$ for $\Lambda$ over $S$ (a two-dimensional abelian scheme with commutative relative group law, an action of $\Lambda$ whose induced traces on tangent spaces are prescribed by reduced traces, and the level-$N$ data of the structure `FakeEllipticCurve`), a morphism $\operatorname{Spec} S \to \mathcal X$ over $s$. The hypothesis `h𝒳` asserts that this is a coarse moduli datum: $\mathrm{pt}$ is invariant under isomorphism of fake elliptic curves, compatible with base change along ring homomorphisms over $\mathcal O$, surjective and injective on points with values in algebraically closed fields, and universal among such assignments. Two further hypotheses require the projection $\mathcal X \times_{\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O} \operatorname{Spec} K_0 \to \operatorname{Spec} K_0$ to be geometrically reduced and geometrically connected.
--
--   The Hecke tower. For each prime $\ell$ different from $r$ and $\bar r$ there are a scheme $\mathcal Y_\ell$ with structure morphism $g_\ell$ to $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and an assignment $\mathrm{pt}_T$ on pairs consisting of a fake elliptic curve of level $N$ together with an extra level-$\ell$ structure (a closed subscheme of the abelian scheme, finite flat of rank $\ell^2$, $\ell$-torsion, stable under $\Lambda$, disjoint from the level-$N$ subscheme, and geometrically isomorphic to $(\mathbb Z/\ell)^2$); `h𝒴` asserts the corresponding coarse moduli property `IsCoarseModuliT` for each $\ell$, and `hgrT`, `hgcT` require each generic fibre projection $\mathcal Y_\ell \times_{\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O}\operatorname{Spec} K_0 \to \operatorname{Spec} K_0$ to be geometrically reduced and geometrically connected. The degeneracy morphisms $d_0^\ell, d_1^\ell : \mathcal Y_\ell \to \mathcal X$ lie over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ (`hd₀f`, `hd₁f`); `hd₀` states that $\mathrm{pt}_T$ of a pair, followed by $d_0^\ell$, is $\mathrm{pt}$ of the underlying curve, and `hd₁` states that if a pair $u$ and a fake elliptic curve $d$ are related by a level-$\ell$ isogeny (an isogeny with kernel the extra level subgroup, $\Lambda$-equivariant, with dual composing to multiplication by $\ell$, and carrying the level-$N$ data) then $\mathrm{pt}_T(u)$ followed by $d_1^\ell$ is $\mathrm{pt}(d)$.
--
--   Atkin–Lehner morphisms. $a_r, a_{\bar r} : \mathcal X \to \mathcal X$ are morphisms over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ such that, on points, $\mathrm{pt}(E)$ followed by $a_r$ (respectively $a_{\bar r}$) equals $\mathrm{pt}(E')$ whenever $E'$ is an Atkin–Lehner quotient of $E$ at $r$ (respectively at $\bar r$) in the sense of `FakeEllipticCurve.IsAtkinLehnerQuotient`.
--
--   The definite quaternion algebra. For rationals $a_1, b_1$, `hdef` states that $a_1 < 0$, $b_1 < 0$, and that $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]\otimes_{\mathbb Q}\mathbb Q_v$ is a division algebra exactly for the finite places $v$ over $\bar r$. In this algebra, written $H$, $\Lambda_1$ is a maximal order, $R_1$ is an Eichler order of level $N$ (an intersection of two maximal orders of relative index $N$) contained in $\Lambda_1$. An idelic unit $n_1 \in (H \otimes_{\mathbb Q} \mathbb A_{\mathbb Q}^{\mathrm{fin}})^\times$ lies in the prime Hecke set of $R_1$ at $r$ (`hn₁`: $n_1$ lies in the adelic box of $R_1$, $r\,n_1^{-1}$ lies in it while $n_1^{-1}$ and $r^{-1}n_1$ do not), and `hS₁` requires the order $R_1 \cap n_1 R_1 n_1^{-1}$ to be Eichler of level $Nr$. A splitting at $r$ is given by an injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra map $\iota_0 : H \to M_2(K_0)$, and $v$ is a height-one prime of the integers of $\mathbb Q$ containing $r$.
--
--   The uniformising group and the Hecke elements. $\tilde\Gamma$ is a subgroup of $H^\times$ whose members are exactly those of [`CerednikDrinfeld.CosetGraph.awayUnits R₁ v`](def/CerednikDrinfeld_CosetGraphAtPrime.html#L37), that is, the elements lying in the subgroup generated by the local unit box of $R_1$ at every height-one prime $w \neq v$. For each prime $\ell$ away from $r$ and $\bar r$ there are $s_\ell \in H^\times$ and an idelic unit $\mathrm{sf}_\ell$; the hypothesis `hs` requires, for each $\ell$: the component of $\mathrm{sf}_\ell$ at each prime not containing $r$ is $s_\ell \otimes 1$; its component at each prime containing $r$ is $1$; the product of the diagonal image of the scalar $\ell$ with $\mathrm{sf}_\ell^{-1}$ lies in the level Hecke set $\mathrm{levelHeckeUSet}\,\Lambda_1\,(R_1 \cap n_1R_1n_1^{-1})\,\ell$ when $\ell \mid N$, and in the prime Hecke set of $R_1 \cap n_1R_1n_1^{-1}$ at $\ell$ otherwise; and $\mathrm{nrd}(s_\ell) = \ell$. Moreover $\tilde\Gamma(\ell) := \tilde\Gamma \cap s_\ell \tilde\Gamma s_\ell^{-1}$ (`hΓtℓ`). Finally $\bar w \in H^\times$ satisfies $\mathrm{nrd}(\bar w) = \bar r$ and normalises $\tilde\Gamma$ (`hwbar`), and some integral multiple $r^{k}\bar w$ lies in $R_1$ (`hwR`).
--
--   Conclusion. There exist families
--   $$\Theta_B : \bigl((O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to_{\mathcal O} B) \times \Omega_{K_0,\pi}(B)\bigr) \longrightarrow \{\varphi : \operatorname{Spec} B \to \mathcal X \mid \varphi \text{ over } \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O\},$$
--   and likewise $\Theta^T_{\ell,B}$ with values in the corresponding set for $g_\ell$, defined for every $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which the image of $\pi$ is nilpotent. Here the source is the product of the functor corepresented by $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ and the functor $\Omega_{K_0,\pi}$ of Deligne data: a Deligne datum over $B$ assigns to each full $\mathcal O$-lattice $M$ in $K_0^2$ a $B$-submodule of $M \otimes_{\mathcal O} B$ with invertible quotient, monotone in $M$, equivariant for homotheties, and nondegenerate at every prime of $B$ in the sense of the structure `DeligneDatum`; the target is the functor `Scheme.nilpPoints` of $B$-points over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$. The twisted action used below is `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct`: for $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$, the relation between $x = (\psi, P)$ and $x' = (\psi', P')$ holds when $\psi' = \psi \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{-v\!\det(g)}$ and $P'$ is the pullback of $P$ along $g^{-1}$. The asserted properties are:
--
--   (i) $\Theta$ is natural: for every $\mathcal O$-algebra homomorphism $\varphi : B \to B'$ between $\mathcal O$-algebras with $\pi$ nilpotent, $\Theta_{B'}$ of the transported point equals the transport of $\Theta_B$; (ii) the same for each $\Theta^T_\ell$.
--
--   (iii) $\Theta$ is invariant under $\tilde\Gamma$: if $\gamma \in \tilde\Gamma$ and $x, x'$ are related by the twisted action of $\iota_0(\gamma)$, then $\Theta_B(x') = \Theta_B(x)$; (iv) the same for $\Theta^T_\ell$ with $\tilde\Gamma(\ell)$ in place of $\tilde\Gamma$.
--
--   (v) For every algebraically closed field $k$ which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra with $\pi$ nilpotent and every $\psi : O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to_{\mathcal O} k$: every $k$-point of $f$ is $\Theta_k(\psi, P)$ for some Deligne datum $P$, and $\Theta_k(\psi,P) = \Theta_k(\psi,P')$ if and only if some $\gamma \in \tilde\Gamma$ carries $(\psi,P)$ to $(\psi,P')$ under the twisted action of $\iota_0(\gamma)$; (vi) the same for each $\Theta^T_\ell$, with $g_\ell$ and $\tilde\Gamma(\ell)$.
--
--   (vii) $\Theta$ is universal for $\tilde\Gamma$-invariant natural families: for every scheme $T$ with a morphism $t$ to $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and every family $\rho$ from the above source functor to the $B$-points of $t$ which is natural and invariant under the twisted action of $\tilde\Gamma$, there is a family $u$ from the $B$-points of $f$ to the $B$-points of $t$, natural in $B$, with $u \circ \Theta = \rho$, and any natural family $u'$ with $u' \circ \Theta = \rho$ agrees with $u$ on all such $B$; (viii) the same for each $g_\ell$, $\Theta^T_\ell$ and $\tilde\Gamma(\ell)$.
--
--   (ix) Compatibility with the first degeneracy map: $\Theta^T_{\ell,B}(x)$ followed by $d_0^\ell$ equals $\Theta_B(x)$. (x) Compatibility with the second: if $x'$ and $x$ are related by the twisted action of $\iota_0(s_\ell)$ (from $x'$ to $x$), then $\Theta^T_{\ell,B}(x)$ followed by $d_1^\ell$ equals $\Theta_B(x')$.
--
--   (xi) The Atkin–Lehner morphism at $r$ acts by the Frobenius twist: $\Theta_B(x)$ followed by $a_r$ equals $\Theta_B(\psi \circ \mathrm{Fr}, P)$ where $x = (\psi, P)$. (xii) The Atkin–Lehner morphism at $\bar r$ acts through $\bar w$: if $x$ and $x'$ are related by the twisted action of $\iota_0(\bar w)$, then $\Theta_B(x)$ followed by $a_{\bar r}$ equals $\Theta_B(x')$.
--
--   This is the Čerednik–Drinfeld $r$-adic uniformisation of the Shimura curve attached to an indefinite quaternion algebra ramified at $r$ and $\bar r$, formulated for the coarse moduli scheme of fake elliptic curves of level $N$ over an absolutely unramified complete discrete valuation ring with residue field $\mathbb F_r$, together with its level-raising tower, its two degeneracy maps and the Atkin–Lehner morphisms at $r$ and $\bar r$, and expressed as an identification of the functor of points on $\pi$-nilpotent algebras with the quotient of Drinfeld's formal half-plane functor (twisted by $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$) by the group of units of an Eichler order in the definite algebra ramified at $\bar r$. It is used to construct the intertwining data at the two places in [`CerednikDrinfeld.exists_descentIntertwiningBase_of_moduliTowerWitness_one_zero_of_two_mul_dvd`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.exists_descentIntertwiningBase_of_moduliTowerWitness_one_zero_of_two_mul_dvd) and its companion with the roles of the two places exchanged.
